# Q2: Pose Candidates Comparison. Rank at least 2 pose configurations (torso_height, arm_position, gripper_orientation, head, left_arm, x/y/z) and state the winner and why—do NOT pick presets from gesture names alone.
# Pose Candidate 1: Start with arm retracted (`arm_position: "back"`, `elbow_bended: true`). Pose Candidate 2: Start with arm already extended (`arm_position: "front"`, `elbow_bended: true`). Winner is Candidate 1 because starting with the arm back creates a dynamic and intentional forward thrust that clearly signals an invitation.
# Q3: Structure Comparison. Compare 2+ primitive sequences (e.g., M1: pose > movement vs M2: pose > path). State the winner and why.
# M1: `pose(retracted)` > `path(extend_arm)` > `movement(nod)`. M2: `pose(extended)` > `movement(nod)`. Winner is M1 because the path-based arm extension from a retracted pose is the core of the "invitation" gesture, making it much more expressive than starting with the fist already out.
# Q4: Context Reinforcement. Compare 2+ small surrounding actions that make the gesture interpretation stronger and pick the best one.
# Option 1: A single, firm head nod after extending the fist. Option 2: A small torso lift during the extension. Winner is Option 1, as the head nod is a direct, friendly social cue that reinforces the invitation to connect, whereas a torso lift might seem overly assertive.
